   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 5 of the laws of 1997 relating to the creation of the\nRoswell Park Cancer Institute corporation and providing for the rights,\npowers, duties and jurisdiction of such corporation, in relation to\nprocurement contracts awarded by the institute",
    "basePrintNo": "S5604",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5604-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §15, Chap 5 of 1997",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5604\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 5 of the laws of 1997 relating\nto the creation of the Roswell Park Cancer Institute corporation and\nproviding for the rights, powers, duties and jurisdiction of such corpo-\nration, in relation to procurement contracts awarded by the institute\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide Roswell Park Cancer Institute\nwith flexibility in its procurement, thereby allowing more resources to\nbe focused on its mission.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends section 15 of chapter 5 of the laws of 1997 to provide\nRoswell Park Cancer Institute a threshold of $125,000 for its discre-\ntionary procurements.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRoswell Park Cancer Institute, although a health care public benefit\ncorporation (\"PBC\"), it is uniquely different than other healthcare PBCs\nsuch as Westchester, Nassau, and Erie Medical Centers because of its\ndistinct education and research enterprises. Medical equipment purchases\nonly represent about 10-15% of the Roswell's contracting activity. The\nmajority of Roswell's contracting involves research, academic and\nprevention services that result in Roswell having a larger contracting\nvolume than its peer PBCs.\n\nThe procurement threshold proposed by this legislation is similar to the\nlower end threshold imposed on SUNY facilities ($125,000).\n\nThis bill provides Roswell with the ability to reduce the significant\nadministrative costs associated with having to issue an RFP for some of\nthe lower valued contracts that Roswell procures.\n\nEach RFP issued costs Roswell an average of $35,000-$40,000 in terms of\nhours to administer.\n\nThis bill will not impact the requirement for Roswell to competitively\nbid its more complicated and resource intensive contracts.\n\nIt is estimated that by adjusting Roswell's procurement threshold, it\ncould save Roswell approximately $500,000 annually, and free up staff\nand resources to continue to pursue the research and academic mission of\nRoswell.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
